From b69106ec3b80cca22990857fa9ac907e8ddd4746 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: suiyan <suiyan@sunmmio.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:14:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] dadao: real CFX exception entry + timer clockevent (KL-155a)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Install a real CFX exception/interrupt entry (entry.S) and a working
cfx_timer-driven clockevent, so jiffies actually advances and Linux
boot gets past calibrate_delay_converge()'s busy-wait (KL-154a's
precisely diagnosed blocker).

- entry.S: two real vector targets sharing a save/dispatch/restore/
  escape body -- dadao_cfx_smon_entry (cfx_smon, cfxcode=2) and
  dadao_cfx_timer_entry (cfx_timer, cfxcode=18). Async TIMER delivery
  targets cfx_timer's OWN vector, not cfx_smon's (verified against
  QEMU's dadao_cfx_async_scan()/dadao_cfx_precise_trap_enter(), which
  target the pending source's own cfxcode). The timer entry's first
  instruction is an atomic self-mask (cfx2rc using rd3, a globally
  LLVM-reserved register preloaded once at trap_init() time) that
  closes the TIMER self-reentrancy window documented in KL-131a's own
  review; save/restore follows the K2-frozen frame protocol
  (KL-140a/142a) exactly, sized/padded to struct pt_regs (0x638 bytes,
  16-byte aligned 0x640 frame for the C ABI).

- traps.c: trap_init() installs both real vectors and a one-time
  reentry guard; dadao_do_smon_trap()/dadao_do_timer_trap() are the C
  dispatch functions called from entry.S. inner_cfx_mask (the live,
  non-storage-backed register gating cross-cfx delivery) has no direct
  cfx2rc write path, so dadao_cfx_craft_inner_mask() uses the same
  self-escape indirection HBI §3's own hypv->supv handoff relies on: a
  self-directed, nonmaskable `trap cfx_smon` whose C handler overwrites
  the frame's prev_cfx_mask before the normal escape epilogue restores
  it. Any other/unexpected cfx_smon or cfx_timer trap fails loudly via
  dadao_die() (show_regs + panic), not silently.

- time.c: cfx_timer counter0 (K1 timer0 profile, one-shot) as a
  CLOCK_EVT_FEAT_ONESHOT clockevent; cfx_hart_cycle_lo as the
  clocksource. set_next_event() re-unmasks TIMER's excp_cause_mask
  gate (which entry.S unconditionally re-blocks on entry) for the next
  event. dadao_timer_interrupt() explicitly brackets the handler with
  local_irq_save/restore, since DADAO hardware -- unlike most
  architectures -- does not implicitly leave "local IRQs disabled" on
  entry (found empirically: kernel/irq_work.c's irq_work_run_list()
  BUG_ON(!irqs_disabled()) firing without it).

- irq.c: arch_local_irq_save/restore now really gate hardware delivery
  via <mode>_global_cfx_mask (the coarse, per-mode-shared gate KL-131a
  already defined for exactly this), not just a software flag.
  dadao_irq_permit_cfx() lets a device (only cfx_timer today) register
  itself into the "permitted when enabled" template.

- dadao-cfx.h: shared (cg,rc)/cfxcode constants plus dadao_cfx2rc()/
  dadao_cfx2rd() macros. Both are deliberately plain macros (not even
  __always_inline functions) using __stringify()-based text
  substitution rather than inline-asm "i"/"r" operand printing: this
  target's inline-asm operand printer does not work yet for either
  immediate or register operands (confirmed empirically -- every
  substituted operand printed as empty text), and separately, -O0
  build (KL-153a/dadao_defconfig) does not reliably constant-fold
  literals through a function-parameter boundary for "i" constraints
  even with __always_inline. Register-operand writes use the same
  named-register-variable idiom already established for `trap`'s rd16
  payload.

- dadao-m1.h: one new evidence-only marker, DADAO_M1_PROGRESS_TIMER_
  IRQ_COUNT, an incrementing (non-magic) count of real timer
  interrupts serviced -- direct evidence the CFX async dispatch ->
  cfx_timer real entry -> clockevent path fires repeatedly, not just
  that jiffies happened to change once for an unrelated reason.

QEMU invocation requirement (not a source workaround, documented in
time.c and the task record): this timer is only reliably interruptible
under `-icount shift=0`. Without it, QEMU's normal TCG direct
TB-chaining can keep re-executing an already-cached tight backward-
branch loop (e.g. calibrate_delay_converge()'s own busy-wait) without
ever returning to the dispatch loop that reconsiders
cpu_exec_interrupt() -- dadao_translate_code()'s per-translation
single-instruction forcing (gated on cfx_timer_ctrl&ENABLE / a pending
TIMER cause) only takes effect for TBs translated while that condition
already holds, not for an already-cached TB reused afterward. -icount
forces deterministic single-instruction-granularity dispatch,
reliably reconsidering pending interrupts regardless of TB caching.

Syscall dispatch and real copy_thread()/__switch_to() remain out of
scope (KL-156a+); boot reaches rest_init_enter/idle_enter with
rest_init_pid=-ENOSYS exactly as KL-154a's diagnosis predicted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
